---
.title = "Deploy LLMD with Docker",
.description = "Pin a CUDA image and model, verify inference, and retain a rollback path on one host.",
.date = .date("2026-11-16T00:00:00"),
.layout = "content.shtml",
---
Deploy a single LLMD instance on a Linux x86-64 host with an NVIDIA GPU.
Use the [LLMD quickstart]($link.page('llmd/quickstart')) for a first chat
request. This guide uses a local checkpoint and a retained container so you
can reproduce the service and roll it back.

# [1. Prepare the host and model]($heading.id('prepare-the-host-and-model'))

- Verify the NVIDIA driver with `nvidia-smi`. Install Docker and configure the
  NVIDIA Container Toolkit, then check container GPU access with
  [NVIDIA's sample workload](https://docs.nvidia.com/datacenter/cloud-native/container-toolkit/latest/sample-workload.html).
- Choose a device with memory for the checkpoint and its KV cache. The example
  uses `LiquidAI/LFM3-1.7B-FP8`. Check
  [Compatibility]($link.page('reference/compatibility')) for other image
  architectures, backends, and precisions.
- Place the complete checkpoint in a directory for the selected revision. Keep
  that directory available for restarts and rollback. For a remote model URI,
  see [Checkpoint storage]($link.page('llmd/quickstart').ref('checkpoint-storage')).

Record the image digest, model repository and commit or local artifact hash,
model ID, driver and device, and the exact startup flags. Record which
credentials the deployment uses separately from their values.

# [2. Start the container]($heading.id('start-the-container'))

The digest below selects the Linux amd64 image listed under
`20261106.0-cuda`. Replace `/srv/models/lfm3-fp8` with the absolute path to
your checkpoint. The bound port is on the Docker host.

```sh
LLMD_IMAGE='zmlai/llmd@sha256:9c9742eb0eaa2a3c7f6b895b206f7b9a193ef2c11ba6224562b69e8cfac08646'
LLMD_MODEL_DIR='/srv/models/lfm3-fp8'

docker pull "$LLMD_IMAGE"
docker run -d --name llmd --restart unless-stopped \
  --gpus=all --shm-size=256GB \
  -p 127.0.0.1:8000:8000 \
  --mount "type=bind,source=${LLMD_MODEL_DIR},target=/model,readonly" \
  "$LLMD_IMAGE" --model=/model --model-name=lfm3
```

Use the [image registry](https://hub.docker.com/r/zmlai/llmd/tags) to select a
digest for another release or architecture. This command requests every
available NVIDIA GPU and sets the `/dev/shm` limit from the published CUDA
recipe. To select devices or change the server's context and memory limits,
see the [CLI reference]($link.page('llmd/cli')). The container and its logs
remain available after it exits. Docker's restart policy starts the container
again after an unexpected exit, subject to
[restart policy behavior](https://docs.docker.com/engine/containers/start-containers-automatically/).

# [3. Verify readiness]($heading.id('verify-readiness'))

Watch the startup output while LLMD loads the weights and compiles the model.
Check the HTTP service, the advertised model ID, and a completion in that
order.

```sh
docker logs --tail 100 llmd
curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8000/health
curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/models
```

`/health` should return `OK`, and `/v1/models` should list `lfm3`. Use that ID
in the request. If you used another `--model-name`, use the ID returned by your
server.

```sh
curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/chat/completions \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
    "model": "lfm3",
    "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Reply with a short greeting."}],
    "max_completion_tokens": 64
  }'
```

Confirm the response contains text in `choices[0].message.content` before
routing traffic. See [Monitoring and troubleshooting]($link.page('operations/monitoring'))
if startup stalls, the model ID differs, or generation fails.

# [4. Route client traffic]($heading.id('route-client-traffic'))

For local clients, keep the host mapping on `127.0.0.1`. For remote clients,
route the chat and model endpoints through a reverse proxy that terminates TLS
and authenticates requests. Restrict backend access to the proxy and restrict
metrics and profiling endpoints to operators. Check your Docker Engine's
[port publishing behavior](https://docs.docker.com/engine/network/port-publishing/)
when configuring the host network.

Set proxy timeouts for your expected inference duration and allow streaming
events to pass through as they arrive. Repeat the chat request through the
client-facing endpoint. See the [API reference]($link.page('llmd/api')) for
streaming request and response formats.

# [5. Upgrade or roll back]($heading.id('upgrade-or-roll-back'))

Record the previous image digest, checkpoint revision, and launch options.
Pull the candidate digest before the maintenance window. On a spare host or
GPU, start the candidate with a distinct container name and port, then repeat
the readiness checks and compare representative requests. If the same GPU is
needed, drain client traffic before stopping the previous container.

For an in-place replacement, stop the running container and reserve the name
`llmd-previous` for rollback. Use a different name for any older retained
container before this step.

```sh
docker stop --timeout 30 llmd
docker rename llmd llmd-previous
```

Start the candidate with the launch command above using its digest and
checkpoint. Restore traffic only after the model ID and chat completion
checks succeed. Retain the previous container and its checkpoint until the
upgrade is accepted.

To roll back, drain traffic again. If the candidate container exists, stop it
and remove it before restoring the retained container.

```sh
docker stop --timeout 30 llmd
docker rm llmd
```

```sh
docker rename llmd-previous llmd
docker start llmd
```

Repeat the readiness checks against the restored instance. The commands are
source-reviewed. Deployment and rollback execution on the described host
remain pending.

# [Next steps]($heading.id('next-steps'))

[Monitor and troubleshoot LLMD]($link.page('operations/monitoring')) to scrape
metrics, check accelerator usage, and diagnose failures.
